CANNOT DETERMINE precisely whether the panel supports a smooth monotonic rise of exactly 1.2-1.3 decades across the full sweep, but it does support the qualitative direction claimed. The panel's fitted activation energies step from 0.73/0.74 eV (δ = 0.032/0.035) to 0.79/0.79 eV (δ = 0.039/0.042), i.e., barrier height increases with δ, which — combined with the separately stated ~2-decade rise in the pre-exponential factor — is broadly consistent with vacancy mobility rising with δ at low temperatures like 50–100 °C, since a larger pre-exponential term would dominate over a modestly larger barrier at low T. However, the caption gives no pre-exponential values per condition, and notably the two highest-δ conditions (0.039 and 0.042) share an identical activation energy (0.79 eV), so any mobility difference between them at 50–100 °C must come entirely from the pre-exponential factor, which this panel description does not specify. Therefore the panel is consistent with the claimed direction of the trend but does not itself verify that the rise is strictly monotonic and of the stated 1.2–1.3-decade magnitude across every step of the full δ sweep.